## Proof

We establish the multidimensional inversion formula. We prove it for the substantive case $a_k\le b_k$ for all $k$ (the case $a_k=b_k$ for some $k$ is trivial: both sides vanish, since $\frac{e^{-it_ka_k}-e^{-it_kb_k}}{it_k}=0$ and $I_{(a_k,a_k)}+\tfrac12I_{\{a_k\}}+\tfrac12I_{\{a_k\}}=0$).

---

### Lemma (1D Dirichlet kernel)

For real $a\le b$, $x\in\mathbb R$, and $c>0$ define
$$
K_c(a,b;x)\;=\;\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-c}^{c}\frac{e^{-ita}-e^{-itb}}{it}\,e^{itx}\,dt .
$$
Then:

(i) $\displaystyle \lim_{c\to\infty}K_c(a,b;x)=I_{(a,b)}(x)+\tfrac12 I_{\{a\}}(x)+\tfrac12 I_{\{b\}}(x)$ for every $x$;

(ii) $\sup_{c>0,\,x\in\mathbb R}|K_c(a,b;x)|\le M$ for a constant $M$ depending only on the kernel (in fact $M$ is an absolute constant, since $M=\sup_{u,R}|\tfrac1\pi\int_0^R\frac{\sin(tu)}{t}dt|<\infty$).

*Proof of Lemma.* Using $\frac{e^{-ita}-e^{-itb}}{it}=\int_a^b e^{-itu}\,du$ (a real quantity, bounded by $b-a$), and the identity
$$
\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-c}^{c}\frac{e^{itu}}{it}\,dt
=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_0^{c}\frac{\sin(tu)}{t}\,dt
\;\xrightarrow[c\to\infty]{}\;\tfrac12\,\mathrm{sgn}(u),
$$
(the cosine part is odd in $t$ and integrates to $0$; the classical Dirichlet integral $\int_0^\infty\frac{\sin v}{v}dv=\frac\pi2$ gives the limit, with value $0$ at $u=0$), we obtain
$$
K_c(a,b;x)=\tfrac12\Bigl[\mathrm{sgn}_c(x-a)-\mathrm{sgn}_c(x-b)\Bigr]
\;\xrightarrow[c\to\infty]{}\;\tfrac12\bigl[\mathrm{sgn}(x-a)-\mathrm{sgn}(x-b)\bigr],
$$
where $\mathrm{sgn}_c(u):=\tfrac2\pi\int_0^c\frac{\sin(tu)}{t}dt$. Evaluating the limit at $x\in(a,b)$, $x<a$, $x>b$, $x=a$, $x=b$ yields exactly $1,0,0,\tfrac12,\tfrac12$, proving (i).

For (ii): $|K_c(a,b;x)|=\tfrac1\pi\bigl|\int_0^c\frac{\sin(t(x-a))-\sin(t(x-b))}{t}dt\bigr|\le \tfrac2\pi\sup_{u,R}|\int_0^R\frac{\sin(tu)}{t}dt|$. The function $\mathrm{Si}(R)=\int_0^R\frac{\sin v}{v}dv$ is bounded on $[0,\infty)$ (it converges to $\pi/2$ with uniformly bounded oscillations), and $\int_0^R\frac{\sin(tu)}{t}dt=\mathrm{sgn}(u)\,\mathrm{Si}(Ru)$, so the supremum over $u,R$ is finite. ∎

---

### Step 1. Rewrite the right–hand side by Fubini

Recall $\varphi(t)=\int_{\mathbb R^n}e^{it\cdot x}\,d\mu(x)$. For fixed $c>0$ the integrand
$$
\prod_{k=1}^n\frac{e^{-it_ka_k}-e^{-it_kb_k}}{it_k}\,e^{it\cdot x},\qquad (t,x)\in[-c,c]^n\times\mathbb R^n,
$$
is bounded in absolute value by $\prod_{k=1}^n|b_k-a_k|$ (using $|\frac{e^{-ita}-e^{-itb}}{it}|=|\int_a^b e^{-ius}du|\le|b-a|$, with the value at $t_k=0$ interpreted as the limit $b_k-a_k$). Hence
$$
\int_{[-c,c]^n}\!\int_{\mathbb R^n}\Bigl|\prod_k\tfrac{e^{-it_ka_k}-e^{-it_kb_k}}{it_k}\,e^{it\cdot x}\Bigr|\,d\mu(x)\,dt
\le \prod_k|b_k-a_k|\,(2c)^n\,\mu(\mathbb R^n)<\infty,
$$
since $\mu$ is a probability measure. Fubini's theorem therefore gives
$$
\frac{1}{(2\pi)^n}\int_{[-c,c]^n}\prod_{k=1}^n\frac{e^{-it_ka_k}-e^{-it_kb_k}}{it_k}\,\varphi(t)\,dt
=\int_{\mathbb R^n}\prod_{k=1}^n K_c(a_k,b_k;x_k)\,d\mu(x).
$$

---

### Step 2. Pass the limit $c\to\infty$ under $\int d\mu$

By the Lemma, for every $x\in\mathbb R^n$,
$$
\prod_{k=1}^n K_c(a_k,b_k;x_k)\;\xrightarrow[c\to\infty]{}\;\prod_{k=1}^n\Bigl[I_{(a_k,b_k)}(x_k)+\tfrac12 I_{\{a_k\}}(x_k)+\tfrac12 I_{\{b_k\}}(x_k)\Bigr]=:G(x).
$$
Moreover, by part (ii) of the Lemma,
$$
\Bigl|\prod_{k=1}^n K_c(a_k,b_k;x_k)\Bigr|\le M^n\quad\text{for all }c>0,\;x\in\mathbb R^n.
$$
Since $\mu$ is a **finite** measure (a probability measure), the constant $M^n$ is $\mu$-integrable. Dominated convergence yields
$$
\lim_{c\to\infty}\int_{\mathbb R^n}\prod_{k=1}^n K_c(a_k,b_k;x_k)\,d\mu(x)
=\int_{\mathbb R^n}G(x)\,d\mu(x).
$$

---

### Step 3. Conclusion

Combining Steps 1 and 2,
$$
\lim_{c\to\infty}\frac{1}{(2\pi)^n}\int_{[-c,c]^n}\prod_{k=1}^n\frac{e^{-it_ka_k}-e^{-it_kb_k}}{it_k}\,\varphi(t)\,dt
=\int_{\mathbb R^n}\prod_{k=1}^n\Bigl[I_{(a_k,b_k)}+\tfrac{I_{\{a_k\}}+I_{\{b_k\}}}{2}\Bigr]\,d\mu,
$$
which is precisely the asserted multidimensional inversion formula.

The argument uses only: (a) the classical one-dimensional Dirichlet integral and the boundedness of the sine-integral $\mathrm{Si}$, giving the pointwise kernel limit together with a uniform bound; (b) Fubini's theorem, justified by the integrable bound $\prod|b_k-a_k|$ on the finite box $[-c,c]^n$; and (c) dominated convergence, legitimated by the uniform bound $M^n$ together with the finiteness of the probability measure $\mu$. ∎

